# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Install to /etc/radar/alert.env (root-owned, 0640, group guardian).
#
#   sudo install -m 0640 -o root -g guardian alert.env.example /etc/radar/alert.env
#   sudo nano /etc/radar/alert.env
#
# radar-brief.service already loads it: `EnvironmentFile=-/etc/radar/alert.env`,
# and the leading `-` means a missing file is not an error. So the resting state
# is "no webhook, journal only", which is rule 8 -- absent config never turns a
# failure into a success. `radar brief` still writes one journal line per
# failing check at error priority whatever is set here:
#
#   journalctl -t radar-brief -p err -n 20
#
# --- Why a page cannot be the alarm ------------------------------------------
#
# The failure this exists for is the recorder exiting at 05:37 and nobody
# noticing for thirteen hours (LEARNINGS 8). A dashboard has exactly the
# property that caused it: somebody has to decide to look. The sibling service
# on this box has been writing alerts to an unread log for months for the same
# reason. A public status page is worth building for *users*; it cannot be the
# thing that wakes an operator.

# Where to POST when a check fails. Unset means nothing is posted.
#
# Four destinations are supported and none of them needs a server:
#
#   Telegram     https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/sendMessage
#                RADAR_ALERT_FORMAT=telegram, and RADAR_ALERT_CHAT_ID below.
#                **The recommended one.** The bot token is a real secret rather
#                than a guessable name, delivery is to an app already on the
#                phone, and the history is searchable later -- which matters
#                when the question is "when did this start".
#                Setup, about five minutes and all of it in the app: message
#                @BotFather, `/newbot`, keep the token; then message your new
#                bot once (a bot cannot open a conversation), and read the id
#                out of
#                  curl -s https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates
#                as `result[0].message.chat.id`. A personal chat id is a
#                positive integer; a group's is negative, and both work.
#
#   ntfy.sh      https://ntfy.sh/<a-long-random-topic-name>
#                RADAR_ALERT_FORMAT=text. No account at all -- the fastest to
#                set up and the weakest: the topic name IS the credential, so
#                anyone who guesses it reads the alerts and can post to them.
#                Make it long and random. The content is "radar is unhealthy",
#                which is not a secret, but the noise would be yours.
#
#   Discord      a channel webhook URL from Server Settings > Integrations.
#                Leave RADAR_ALERT_FORMAT unset; the JSON body carries
#                `content`, which is the field Discord reads.
#
#   Slack        an incoming-webhook URL. Also the default format; Slack reads
#                `text` and ignores `content`.
#
# RADAR_ALERT_WEBHOOK=

# json (default)  {"text": ..., "content": ...}  Slack reads one field, Discord
#                                               the other, each ignoring the
#                                               one it does not know.
# text            the message as a plain body   ntfy.sh.
# telegram        {"chat_id": ..., "text": ...} Telegram's own shape. It needs
#                                               the chat id below; without it
#                                               nothing is sent and the journal
#                                               says why, because a curl that
#                                               succeeds while delivering to
#                                               nobody is the worst outcome
#                                               available here.
#
# An unrecognised value is treated as json rather than guessed at.
#
# RADAR_ALERT_FORMAT=telegram

# Telegram only: which chat to deliver to. Required by RADAR_ALERT_FORMAT
# =telegram and ignored by every other format.
#
# RADAR_ALERT_CHAT_ID=

# Overrides for the check itself, both already defaulted in the script. Set the
# serving URL only if radar-serve moves off 127.0.0.1:8402.
#
# RADAR_STORE=/home/guardian/radar/data/store
# RADAR_SERVE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8402
